    <!-- FAQ Section -->
    <div class="relative bg-white py-24 sm:py-32" id="queues-faq">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6 lg:px-8">
        <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl text-center mb-16">
          <p class="text-sm font-medium text-indigo-600 uppercase tracking-wide mb-3">FAQ</p>
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-gray-900 sm:text-4xl text-balance">
            Questions platform teams ask
          </h2>
        </div>

        <dl class="divide-y divide-gray-200">
          <div class="py-8">
            <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Do I have to install anything on the broker?</dt>
            <dd class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">No agent of ours. Queues come from the messaging spans your instrumented applications already send. Broker health comes from a stock <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">otel/opentelemetry-collector-contrib</code> collector reading the broker's management API or metrics endpoint, or your cloud provider's monitoring API. Some brokers need their metrics switched on first: RabbitMQ's management plugin, RocketMQ's Prometheus exporter or NATS's monitoring port, and ActiveMQ takes the OpenTelemetry JMX Scraper beside it.</dd>
          </div>

          <div class="py-8">
            <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">What access does the collector need?</dt>
            <dd class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Enough to read the broker's statistics, and no more. On RabbitMQ that is a user tagged <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">monitoring</code>, given access to each virtual host with empty permission patterns, so it can neither configure, publish nor consume. On AWS it is <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">cloudwatch:ListMetrics</code> and <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">cloudwatch:GetMetricData</code>, on Azure the <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">Monitoring Reader</code> role, and on Google Cloud the <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">Monitoring Viewer</code> role. Kafka's receiver connects to the brokers like any client, with SASL and TLS when your cluster uses them.</dd>
          </div>

          <div class="py-8">
            <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Will every reply queue and test script become a queue?</dt>
            <dd class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">No. Traces create a queue only once 3 of its spans arrive in 15 minutes, so a one-off script does not make one. Temporary and anonymous destinations, server-named reply queues and inboxes are never queues, and a UUID inside a name is folded into <code class="text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">{uuid}</code>. Automatic creation stops at 500 live discovered queues per project, and an untouched discovered queue nobody has seen for 7 days is archived on its own.</dd>
          </div>

          <div class="py-8">
            <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">We run two Kafka clusters with a topic of the same name. Are they two queues?</dt>
            <dd class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">No, they share one. A Kafka topic, a RabbitMQ queue or an SQS queue is keyed by its system and name alone, because spans rarely name the cluster they come from: putting the cluster in the key would split one topic by instrumentation instead. Azure Service Bus and Event Hubs are the exception. Their namespace is part of the queue, since both the SDKs and Azure Monitor name it reliably.</dd>
          </div>

          <div class="py-8">
            <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">How do I alert on a queue?</dt>
            <dd class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Open the queue and press Create monitor beside a broker health chart: it opens a <a href="/docs/monitor/metrics-monitor" class="text-indigo-600 hover:text-indigo-700 font-medium">Metrics monitor</a> filtered to that queue, with a starting threshold where the metric has one. To watch every queue of a system with one monitor, group by the attribute that names the queue, and each queue alerts on its own. The monitor routes to on-call, incidents and status pages like any other.</dd>
          </div>

          <div class="py-8">
            <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Do Azure Functions triggered by Service Bus show up?</dt>
            <dd class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Yes. With OpenTelemetry turned on in the Functions host, it exports the Service Bus and Event Hubs processor spans behind triggered functions, and their queues appear from those spans like any other consumer's. The functions themselves, with their invocations, cold starts and errors, are on <a href="/product/serverless" class="text-indigo-600 hover:text-indigo-700 font-medium">Serverless</a>.</dd>
          </div>

          <div class="py-8">
            <dt class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Where do I start?</dt>
            <dd class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Create a project, send OpenTelemetry traces from the services that publish and consume, and open <strong class="font-medium text-gray-800">Resources → Queues</strong>. Each queue's Documentation tab has the setup for its messaging system. The <a href="/docs/telemetry/queues" class="text-indigo-600 hover:text-indigo-700 font-medium">Queues docs</a> cover discovery, instrumenting applications in each language, broker health metrics for every system, alerting and troubleshooting.</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>

